Not really recommended for reading
Read some time ago; I just happened to recall and jot down some reflections.
This book is more a collection of fragments about love from life, rather than a short story collection.
The tone of this book is extremely bland, like plain water that is bland, pursuing simplicity to an extreme in its choice of words and sentences, and the plot is also as quiet and bland as a street on a summer afternoon.
Every short story is a silhouette of a small segment in ordinary life, with no beginning and no end.
Finally collected together, under the title “When We Talk About Love, What Are We Really Talking About?”
I believe that this collection of novels, along with its title, is a form of performance art. The author intends to convey that love is not just the drama of stage performances, the eternal romance of urban legends, nor merely the intimate attachment of "glue-like" closeness. Love transcends these and should not be confined by them either; love also includes the mundanity of everyday life, the monotonous routine day after day, the confusion with life, and the evasion from reality, as well as the uncertainty about the future.
Reading this book, you may recall couples arguing beside you, boring marriages of people you know, and unhappy relationships.
This is precisely why I would not recommend it for aesthetic reasons; both its prose and plot are utterly bland, unless your life is stormy and tumultuous or as sweet as living in a Candy Castle, in which case you might find reading it merely routine and boring.
And on a depth-of-thought level, the everyday plot does not dazzle or expand one’s perspective (which is different from Gabriel García Márquez’s Love in the Time of Cholera), and the short segments do not explore any issues such as what kind of mistake led to the failure of love.
What are the reasons for the breakdown of the protagonist's marriage?
So I wouldn't recommend this book; either reading it or not would have little significance.
The author’s ideas are conveyed more through technique and genre than through the narrative plot, which is also a reason for the poor reading experience of this book.
