Strongly recommend Amazon to stop using EMS!
Five stars for the product, one star for logistics, average three stars.
Don't understand why Amazon is sending EMS. Everyone in the industry knows it's unreliable, after all, computers are valuable items!
After placing the order, the delivery was expected to arrive on the 20th, so my family waited at home for three days!
Why does it take four to five days to send something from Xiamen to Fuzhou?
This morning on the 22nd, I checked the logistics and finally received it in Fuzhou. I was preparing to happily receive the package when, in the evening, without receiving any phone calls or messages, I checked the logistics and found that it had been signed for midday, with even the status showing it as 'personally signed for.'
I was instantly shocked!
Who am I? Where am I? Is this computer really mine?
Damn, it's like doubting life itself!
Finally had to go to the pickup point to get the computer back.
The specific reason, in simple terms, is that the delivery guy mixed up the two tracking numbers; fortunately, the package wasn't lost.
I strongly suggest that Amazon don't use EMS anymore, and I definitely won't buy from them again if they start using EMS for their deliveries.
Awaiting supplier's response
The packaging has no markings; simply call the supplier and say that there is no marking is for a genuine product. If you suspect anything, you can send some scraps to Johnson & Johnson for verification. I left my vehicle plate number and phone number as well, promising to send a text message to notify them to input my information into the Johnson & Johnson warranty card. But I haven’t received any such messages yet?
The packaging is damaged; let time reveal the quality of the product.
Severe damage to the outer packaging, while the inner box seems new. The manual appears not to be new; it was renovated in September and is stored in stock. I hope it won't be bad by the time it arrives. Could Amazon explain why several customers mentioned damaged packaging while you seem unaffected? Thank you!
The things are good, but platform bureaucracy remains.
